      "question": "If $n(A)=12$, $n(B)=15$, $n(A \\cup B)=23$, find $n(A \\cap B)$ and discuss $n(A' \\cap B')$.",
      "answer": "$n(A \\cap B)=4$; $n(A' \\cap B')$ needs $n(U)$ (would be 0 if $A \\cup B = U$).",
      "options": [],
      "glassboxSteps": [
        {
          "rule": "Intersection",
          "why": "Rearrange union formula.",
          "math": "n(A \\cap B) = 12 + 15 - 23 = 4"
        },
        {
          "rule": "$n(A' \\cap B')$",
          "why": "$= n(U) - n(A \\cup B)$; $n(U)$ not given.",
          "math": "n(A' \\cap B') = n(U) - 23"
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "$n(A\\cap B)=4$; $n(A'\\cap B')$ requires $n(U)$.",
      "linked_note_id": "CH06-sec-operations-on-sets",
      "source": "practice-session"
